WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.220
In 2026, more than fifteen thousand people

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.860
applied to Google Summer of Code.

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:08.082
If you're thinking about trying,

00:00:08.122 --> 00:00:09.842
you've probably wondered:

00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:13.042
Am I too late? Do I know enough?

00:00:13.082 --> 00:00:14.682
Where do I even start?

00:00:17.844 --> 00:00:22.664
In 2026, 183 organizations took part.

00:00:22.704 --> 00:00:25.564
It's easy to end up somewhere that's gone quiet,

00:00:25.604 --> 00:00:28.434
under an issue five people already claimed.

00:00:30.288 --> 00:00:33.228
git into gsoc is a free, open-source guide

00:00:33.268 --> 00:00:34.888
for exactly this moment.

00:00:35.008 --> 00:00:37.288
It even tells you where you are in the year,

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:39.038
and what to do now.

00:00:40.728 --> 00:00:42.738
It starts with four questions.

00:00:42.778 --> 00:00:46.038
What you know. What you'd enjoy.

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.038
How much time you have.

00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:50.288
And how much open source you've done.

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:56.754
Then it shows you where you'd fit, and why.

00:00:56.794 --> 00:00:59.174
Every reason names its source.

00:00:59.214 --> 00:01:02.174
It tells you how well the work fits you.

00:01:02.214 --> 00:01:04.034
Never your odds of getting in.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.780
Pick one, like stdlib, and the terminal reads

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.460
what a website can't: whether people outside the team get merged,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.880
how fast maintainers reply,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.760
and how big a change tends to land.

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:23.159
Before you claim an issue,

00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:24.839
it checks whether someone got there first.

00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:27.159
And it reminds you what maintainers hope to read.

00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:29.729
Not "Can I work on this?"

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:33.279
But what you'd change, and why.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.382
Rather ask in your own words?

00:01:38.452 --> 00:01:41.602
The same tools work inside AI assistants like Claude Code,

00:01:41.642 --> 00:01:43.572
and they show their sources.

00:01:43.612 --> 00:01:46.202
The tools never post anything for you.

00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:50.565
So whenever you feel stuck,

00:01:50.895 --> 00:01:53.085
there's one next step you can take today.

00:01:57.237 --> 00:01:58.467
Open source has a door.

00:02:01.437 --> 00:02:02.837
Find yours at gitintogsoc.me.
